ORIGIN

  https://rubygems.org/downloads/nio4r-2.7.5.gem
  sha256 6c90168e48fb5f8e768419c93abb94ba2b892a1d0602cb06eef16d8b7df1dca1

  nio4r bundles libev 4.x (ext/libev), maintained by Marc Lehmann; see
  ext/libev/LICENSE (BSD-2-Clause / GPL-2.0+ dual).

WHY THE DIRECTORY LAYOUT LOOKS REDUNDANT

  The gem's own tree (ext/nio4r/ and ext/libev/) is preserved verbatim under
  this directory, so the paths are third_party/ruby/ext/nio4r/ext/nio4r/*.c
  and third_party/ruby/ext/nio4r/ext/libev/*.  That is deliberate:
  ext/nio4r/nio4r_ext.c does `#include "../libev/ev.c"`, and keeping the
  layout is what makes this a zero-patch vendoring.

LOCAL CHANGES

  - ext/nio4r/*.{c,h} + extconf.rb -> ext/nio4r/ (unmodified)
  - ext/libev/*                    -> ext/libev/ (unmodified)
  - lib/**                         -> lib/ (unmodified)
  - license.md                     -> here
  - ext/nio4r/org/**.java (JRuby backend) is not vendored.
  - extconf.rb is kept for reference only; its have_header()/have_func()
    results and its libev backend selection are hand transcribed into
    BUILD.mk.  See BUILD.mk for why the poll(2) backend was chosen.
  - nio4r.gemspec is hand-written (upstream's declares `extensions`).

  No .c or .h file has been modified.

RUNTIME NOTE

  lib/nio.rb picks the pure-Ruby Kernel.select engine when NIO.pure? is true,
  which it decides from Gem.win_platform?.  Under CosmoRuby RUBY_PLATFORM is
  "x86_64-cosmo"/"aarch64-cosmo", so win_platform? is false and the C
  extension is used on every host, Windows included -- cosmopolitan's poll()
  works there (libc/sock/poll-nt.c).  Setting NIO4R_PURE=true in the
  environment forces the pure-Ruby engine if that ever needs a workaround.
